# Changes

## Unreleased

### Release hardening: conservative parser semantics and bounded copies (2026-08)

- JavaScript is byte-identical by default; its historical byte scanner now
  requires `strip_aggressive on;` because regex lexical goals, escaped
  identifiers, numeric accessors, and nested templates need a real parser.
- CSS hex shortening is aggressive-only so an ID selector such as `#aabbcc`
  is never rewritten to a different selector by default.
- HTML/SVG/XML preserve all literal character-data whitespace by default.
  Removing a comment concatenates its surrounding text without inventing a
  space, matching the parsed character data.
- HTML raw/RCDATA protection now includes `title`, `iframe`, `xmp`, `noembed`,
  and `noframes` in addition to `pre`, `textarea`, `script`, and `style`.
- Known bodies above `strip_max_size` bypass in the header filter, before any
  body-sized allocations. Minification reuses the coalesced input buffer in
  place, reducing peak module-owned storage for unknown/chunked bodies from
  roughly three body copies to two. The default cap is reduced from 10 MiB to
  1 MiB; deployments that intentionally minify larger bodies can set it back.
- Added live nginx coverage for custom `strip_types`, known oversize and
  encoded bypasses, and ETag weakening, plus in-place and parser regressions.

### Native Windows build coverage (2026-08)

Added native Windows support gates for both nginx toolchains: an MSVC x64
static build (`--add-module`) and a MinGW-w64 x64 dynamic build
(`--add-dynamic-module`). Both jobs verify module registration, test a real
directive plus a bogus-directive negative control, and compare raw and filtered
in-memory HTML responses. Source and CI line endings are pinned to LF so the
same module manifest and shell tooling work in Unix, MSYS2, and nginx's MSVC
build.

### Documented limitation: whole-body buffering, flush not honoured (2026-08)

Recorded as a known and intentional constraint rather than changed: the filter
buffers the whole response body before minifying, and ignores `flush`/`sync`
buffers that carry no terminal flag. Streamed and SSE-style responses are held
until complete. The minifier carries no lexer state across calls, so emitting
early would corrupt comments or strings spanning the buffer seam. Buffering is
bounded only in size: once the accumulated body exceeds `strip_max_size` bytes
the filter passes the remainder through. That is a byte threshold, not a
streaming switch, so a stream that stays under the limit is still held until it
completes; use `strip off;` on those locations. A resumable minifier that could
honour flush is tracked as future work.

### BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: first conservative-mode gates (2026-08)

Gated four transforms — proven able to corrupt valid input under some inputs
— behind `STRIP_F_AGGRESSIVE` / `strip_aggressive on;` (default remains
`off`):

- CSS: zero-unit stripping (`0px` → `0`) is invalid inside contexts where a
  bare `<number>` is not a valid `<length>`, e.g.
  `@media (min-width:0px)` → `(min-width:0)`.
- JS: dropping the space between two identical operator characters changes
  the token stream, e.g. `a + +b` → `a++b` (unary plus fused into increment).
- SVG/XML: collapsing text-node whitespace has no `xml:space="preserve"`
  awareness and can corrupt whitespace-significant content.
- HTML: collapsing text-node whitespace has no `white-space: pre`/`pre-wrap`
  (CSS) awareness and can corrupt whitespace-significant content.

This was the first conservative-mode pass. The release-hardening entry above
extends the gate to JavaScript as a whole, CSS hex shortening, and all
inter-element HTML/SVG/XML character data. `output <= input` still holds.

### `strip_aggressive` directive: opt-in for lossy minification (2026-08)

Added the `strip_aggressive on|off;` directive (default `off`) and threaded its
flag from nginx configuration through the standalone core. Subsequent entries
above describe the transforms now gated by it.

### CI standardisation / skeleton-layout adoption (2026-08)

Adopted the `nginx-skeleton-module` reference CI layout: sources moved to
`src/`, tests/fuzz/tools consolidated under `ci/`, and the workflow set
(`ci.yml`, `build-test.yml`, `security-scanners.yml`, `fuzzing.yml`,
`asan.yml`, `codeql.yml`, `valgrind.yml`, `ci-deep.yml`, `bump.yml`) rebuilt
around a single `ci.yml` PR-lane entry point instead of five independent
`pull_request` triggers. Along the way:

- Local lint gate ported to `ci/linter/` (`run-all.sh` + `install.sh`),
  covering flawfinder, semgrep, shellcheck, actionlint, `ruff` (pinned
  `0.16.1`), `lint-ci-ports.sh` (every runtime-bearing CI job declares a
  `TEST_BASE_PORT`), and now `lint-docs-drift.sh` (README `## CI` table and
  `.github/workflows/` never drift apart).
- `.pre-commit-config.yaml` and `.githooks/pre-commit` tracked (previously
  present but untracked, so a fresh clone got zero hooks); `core.hooksPath`
  wiring documented in `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
- `valgrind.yml` deliberately moved out of the PR lane to weekly
  cron + `workflow_dispatch` (retains `workflow_call`) — it was the 769s
  budget-setter on every PR (2.5x the rest of the critical path combined).
  PR-lane wall-clock: 12m52s -> 5m59s. Per-PR memory-safety coverage remains
  via `asan.yml`. Depth of the weekly/on-demand run is unchanged. See
  `memory/labs/nginx-strip-filter-module/skeleton-findings.md` § F-VG.
- Bot-review sweep across PRs 1-16, 18, 20-44: 24 review + 30 issue-level
  `coderabbitai[bot]` comments triaged, 9 confirmed findings fixed, 2 refuted
  with evidence, 3 already fixed in-tree.
- Extra target-only gates kept (not folded into the reference set): the
  `unit-core` job (standalone `strip_core.c` unit run, no nginx build), 6
  fuzz targets (one per content type vs. the reference's 2), the Discord
  failure-notify steps in `ci-deep.yml`, and `ci/tools/sync-stamp.sh`
  (detects drift between this repo's shared `.github/` files and the
  reference skeleton's).

See `memory/labs/nginx-strip-filter-module/` (index.md, issues.md,
lessons.md, skeleton-findings.md, adoption-findings.md) for full detail,
measurements and rejected alternatives.
